-- title:
--   A finitely generated convex cone is closed
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a finite-dimensional real normed space and let $u : \kappa \to F$ be a finite family of vectors. Then the cone they generate,
--
--   $$
--   \operatorname{cone}(u) \;=\; \Big\{\, \textstyle\sum_{k} c_k\, u_k \;:\; c \ge 0 \,\Big\},
--   $$
--
--   is a closed subset of $F$.
--
--   This is the one genuinely topological ingredient of linear programming duality, and it is not
--   obvious: the image of the closed set $\{c \ge 0\}$ under a linear map need not be closed in
--   general, and a cone generated by *infinitely* many vectors need not be closed either. What
--   makes the finite case work is Carath\'eodory's theorem for cones: every point of
--   $\operatorname{cone}(u)$ is already a nonnegative combination of a *linearly independent*
--   subfamily. There are finitely many subfamilies; for a linearly independent one the
--   coefficient map is a homeomorphism onto its image, so that piece is closed; and a finite union
--   of closed sets is closed.
--
--   Everything separation-based in linear and semidefinite programming rests on this: Farkas'
--   lemma is the separation of a point from $\operatorname{cone}(u)$, and it needs
--   $\operatorname{cone}(u)$ closed to produce a strictly separating functional.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The cone is written as the set of $\sum_k c_k \cdot u_k$ with
--   $0 \le c$, i.e. with the coefficient family indexed by all of $\kappa$ rather than by a
--   support; the Carath\'eodory reduction is carried out as a descent on the cardinality of a
--   `Finset` of indices.
-- source:
--   Carried as an internal support lemma, with this exact statement and proof, in twelve accepted submissions across ten missions of the OR Formalization Drive (e.g. https://prove2.me/submissions/3c55ced4-4944-4de2-ba43-8110d2682db1); published here once so that it can be imported. Classical; the key topological input to Farkas' lemma. See e.g. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, Theorem 19.1, or Schrijver, Theory of Linear and Integer Programming, §7.

import Mathlib

open Matrix

variable {E : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
variable {ι κ : Type*} [Fintype ι] [Fintype κ]

namespace ConvexCone

theorem isClosed_finite_cone {F : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup F] [NormedSpace ℝ F]
    [FiniteDimensional ℝ F] (u : κ → F) :
    IsClosed {w | ∃ c : κ → ℝ, 0 ≤ c ∧ w = ∑ k, c k • u k} := by sorry

end ConvexCone
